From: Adriel Santos <adriel@adriel.eu>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath11k: give each CE RX pipe its own page-frag cache

The CE RX rings (CE1 HTT 512, CE2 WMI 32, CE5 pktlog 512 entries per
device; 256 for CE5 in the NSS 256 MiB profile) are filled with
dev_alloc_skb(2048), i.e. 2432-byte pieces of the shared per-CPU 32 KiB
page-frag chunks. Ring buffers can wait minutes for the target, while
traffic skbs carved from the same chunks come and go around them; every
buffer left behind pins its whole chunk. Without the skb recycler that the
v1.x kernel had (which turned these into slab objects), the RD03v2 bench
measured 22.6 MiB of untracked pages released by unloading ath11k with
NSS offload on (rc1), against 4.2 MiB on v1.14-nss; the allocation
profiler put 20.7 MiB of it in page-frag chunks after 15 min of idle.

Allocate them from a page-frag cache private to the pipe, under the
ce_lock that already serialises the refill: a chunk then only holds
buffers of one ring and is released once they have been consumed. The
same idea as kuncy7's 999-993 (Georg Seema) for the RXDMA rings, which
only applies inside the optional NSS 256 MiB profile; this covers the CE
rings in every mode. The cache is drained when the pipe is cleaned up.

Measured on the bench on top of the NSS 256 MiB profile and the per-CPU
list cap (same rc1 image, ath11k.ko and ath11k_ahb.ko from one build):
5.1 / 5.3 / 5.1 MiB fewer raw pages at 5 / 15 / 25 min of idle, MemAvailable
40.8 / 39.5 / 39.5 MiB instead of 35.7 / 34.3 / 34.4 MiB. Both radios up,
no warnings. Not yet run with Wi-Fi clients or through crash recovery.

The new member changes struct ath11k_ce_pipe, which struct ath11k_base embeds: ath11k_ahb.ko
and ath11k_pci.ko must come from the same build as ath11k.ko (a bench test that swapped
ath11k.ko alone crashed the board at radio bring-up).

Signed-off-by: Adriel Santos <adriel@adriel.eu>
---
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/ce.c b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/ce.c
index 2d80b6c..1307b37 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/ce.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/ce.c
@@ -319,6 +319,34 @@ exit:
 	return ret;
 }
 
+/* CE RX buffers sit in their ring until the target writes into them, which on
+ * the pktlog and HTT pipes can take minutes, while traffic skbs carved from the
+ * same shared per-CPU page-frag caches come and go around them. Each one left
+ * behind pins its whole 32 KiB chunk, so a few hundred idle ring buffers spread
+ * over many chunks. A private cache per pipe keeps a ring's buffers together:
+ * a chunk holds only buffers of one ring and is freed once they are consumed.
+ */
+static struct sk_buff *ath11k_ce_rx_alloc_skb(struct ath11k_ce_pipe *pipe)
+{
+	unsigned int len = SKB_HEAD_ALIGN(pipe->buf_sz + NET_SKB_PAD);
+	struct sk_buff *skb;
+	void *data;
+
+	data = page_frag_alloc(&pipe->rx_frag_cache, len,
+			       GFP_ATOMIC | __GFP_NOWARN);
+	if (!data)
+		return NULL;
+
+	skb = build_skb(data, len);
+	if (!skb) {
+		page_frag_free(data);
+		return NULL;
+	}
+
+	skb_reserve(skb, NET_SKB_PAD);
+	return skb;
+}
+
 static int ath11k_ce_rx_post_pipe(struct ath11k_ce_pipe *pipe)
 {
 	struct ath11k_base *ab = pipe->ab;
@@ -331,7 +359,7 @@ static int ath11k_ce_rx_post_pipe(struct ath11k_ce_pipe *pipe)
 
 	spin_lock_bh(&ab->ce.ce_lock);
 	while (pipe->rx_buf_needed) {
-		skb = dev_alloc_skb(pipe->buf_sz);
+		skb = ath11k_ce_rx_alloc_skb(pipe);
 		if (!skb) {
 			ret = -ENOMEM;
 			goto exit;
@@ -847,6 +875,11 @@ static void ath11k_ce_rx_pipe_cleanup(struct ath11k_ce_pipe *pipe)
 				 skb->len + skb_tailroom(skb), DMA_FROM_DEVICE);
 		dev_kfree_skb_any(skb);
 	}
+
+	/* Buffers still out keep their own page references */
+	spin_lock_bh(&ab->ce.ce_lock);
+	page_frag_cache_drain(&pipe->rx_frag_cache);
+	spin_unlock_bh(&ab->ce.ce_lock);
 }
 
 static void ath11k_ce_shadow_config(struct ath11k_base *ab)
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/ce.h b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/ce.h
index bcde2fc..a3166ec 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/ce.h
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/ce.h
@@ -7,6 +7,8 @@
 #ifndef ATH11K_CE_H
 #define ATH11K_CE_H
 
+#include <linux/page_frag_cache.h>
+
 #define CE_COUNT_MAX 12
 
 /* Byte swap data words */
@@ -180,6 +182,8 @@ struct ath11k_ce_pipe {
 	struct ath11k_ce_ring *dest_ring;
 	struct ath11k_ce_ring *status_ring;
 	u64 timestamp;
+	/* RX buffers of this pipe only; protected by ce_lock */
+	struct page_frag_cache rx_frag_cache;
 };
 
 struct ath11k_ce {
